course: everyday
lesson: 285
title: Good pressure, good and bad secrets, and owning up
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Peer pressure is not always bad. When friends encourage you to join a team or try a new food, it can help you grow, as long as it is safe.
explain: There are two kinds of secrets. A good secret, like a surprise party or a hidden present, ends happily and nobody gets hurt. Private things a friend trusts you with, like wetting the bed sometimes, are also kept, because sharing them would only embarrass them.
explain: A bad secret is one that hides harm or danger. If someone touches you in a way that feels wrong and says «it's our secret», or a friend talks about bringing a weapon to school, tell a trusted adult straight away. Secrets like these are never okay to keep, and it is never your fault.
explain: Example: a boy in Vancouver knocks over his grandma's vase. He feels scared, but he goes straight to her and says sorry. She is sad about the vase but proud of him for telling her. Adults are usually less upset about honesty than about lies.
explain: Owning up early is easier than later: a lost jacket is easier to find on the first day, and a library book is easier to replace before the fines grow.
fact: Positive peer pressure, like friends encouraging you to join a team or try a new food, can help you grow if it is safe.
fact: A good secret, like a surprise for someone, ends happily and nobody gets hurt.
fact: If someone touches you in a way that feels wrong and says «it's our secret», tell a trusted adult as soon as you can; it is never your fault.
fact: Secrets about touching are never okay to keep, and trusted adults will help.
fact: Keep a friend's private, harmless worries, like wetting the bed, to yourself; sharing them would embarrass them.
fact: If a friend shows you a weapon or says they will bring one to school, tell an adult right away; it is a danger to everyone.
fact: If you find out about a present, keep quiet and act surprised; spoiling it takes the joy from the giver.
fact: If you break something, tell an adult straight away and say sorry; adults are usually less upset about honesty than about lies.
fact: If you lose a library book, tell the librarian; you may need to pay or replace it, and honesty lets the problem be fixed.
fact: If you accidentally hurt a friend, stop, check they are okay and say sorry; caring about them matters more than whose fault it was.
fact: Owning up to small things shows you are trustworthy in big things too.
fact: If you lose something a friend lent you, tell them quickly and help find or replace it; waiting makes it harder.
exercise: Your friends keep encouraging you to join the swimming club, and it is safe. What is this? | Good peer pressure that can help you grow | Bad pressure that you must always refuse, whatever it is | Bullying that you should report | A dare that could hurt you
exercise: Your friends say «Just try one bite of sushi!». You are a bit unsure but it is safe. What is reasonable? | Give it a try, since it is safe | Refuse because all pressure is bad | Tell a teacher they are bullying you | Eat the whole plate to prove yourself
exercise: A friend tells you she is planning a surprise breakfast for her mum. Should you keep it? | Yes, it is a good secret that ends happily | No, you must tell her mum about it today so she knows | No, all secrets are wrong | Only if she pays you
exercise: What makes a surprise a good secret? | It ends happily and nobody gets hurt | Only children know about it | It lasts for a very long time | Grown-ups are not allowed to know it
exercise: Someone touches you in a way that feels wrong and says «This is our secret». What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult as soon as you can | Keep the secret, because you were told to by a grown-up | Wait to see if it happens again | Only tell a friend your age
exercise: If someone tells you to keep a touching secret, whose fault is it? | Never yours; trusted adults will help | Yours, for not saying no loudly enough at the time | Yours, if you agreed to keep it | Nobody's, so forget about it
exercise: A friend tells you he sometimes wets the bed and asks you not to tell. What is right? | Keep it private, as it is personal and harmless | Tell the whole class, so that everybody can help him stop | Tell his older brother as a joke | Write it in your diary for others
exercise: A boy shows you a knife in his bag and says he'll bring it to school tomorrow. What do you do? | Tell an adult right away | Keep it secret to stay his friend | Tell him to hide it better | Wait to see if he really brings it
exercise: You found the bike your parents are hiding for your birthday. What should you do? | Keep quiet and act surprised | Ride it around the garden | Tell them you already know, so they don't need to hide it | Ask for a different colour
exercise: You knock over a vase and it breaks. Nobody saw. What is right? | Tell an adult straight away and say sorry | Hide the pieces under the sofa | Blame the cat | Glue it back together quietly and hope that nobody notices
exercise: Why are adults often less upset when you own up quickly? | They value honesty more than the broken thing | They don't care about vases | It means they can blame someone else instead of you | They are busy and forget
exercise: You lost a book from the school library. What should you do? | Tell the librarian and help pay or replace it | Say you already returned it and the library must have lost it | Never go to the library again | Take another book to make it even
exercise: During tag, you push a friend by accident and she falls. What first? | Stop, check if she is okay and say sorry | Keep running so you don't get caught and lose the game of tag | Say it was her fault for being slow | Laugh so she thinks it was funny
exercise: You ate the last biscuit that was saved for your brother. What is right? | Own up and say sorry | Say the dog ate it | Hide the empty packet | Say you didn't know it was his
exercise: Why does owning up about something small matter? | It shows you can be trusted with big things too | Small things don't matter at all | It gets you more biscuits | It only matters if somebody catches you doing it
exercise: You lost the jacket a friend lent you. When should you tell him? | Quickly, and help find or replace it | Only if he asks for it back | After a few weeks of searching alone | Never; just buy a different one
test: A girl's friends cheer her on to try out for the school play, which scares her a bit. Which is TRUE? | This is pressure that can help her grow | All peer pressure is harmful | She must say no to all pressure, even when it's kind | It is the same as a dangerous dare
test: Which secret should you tell a trusted adult about? | A grown-up who says «don't tell anyone about our special touching» | A surprise party for your teacher that the class is planning | Where your dad hid your mum's present | Your friend's crush on a pop star
test: Your friend says «I'm scared of the dark» and asks you not to tell. What is kind? | Keep it private, as it is personal and harmless | Tell everyone so they help him | Tell his sister for a laugh | Post it online to raise awareness and help him get braver
test: A classmate says he will bring his dad's gun to school to scare someone. You promised to keep his secret. What now? | Tell an adult at once; this promise must be broken | Keep the promise, because promises are sacred and must never break | Wait until you see the gun | Tell only your best friend
test: A girl in Perth breaks her mum's favourite mug and is scared. Which choice will most likely end best? | Telling Mum straight away and saying sorry | Hiding the pieces in the bin | Blaming her little brother | Buying a new one with her pocket money and saying nothing
test: You borrowed a friend's book and spilled juice on it. What should you do? | Tell her at once and offer to replace it | Return it quickly and hope she doesn't notice the juice | Keep it and say you lost it | Dry it and say nothing
test: You hit a cricket ball that hits a friend. It was an accident. What matters most? | Checking he is okay and saying sorry | Proving it wasn't your fault | Carrying on with the game | Telling everyone it was his own fault for standing there
test: Why is it better to report a lost item quickly? | It is easier to find and easier to explain | It makes you look clever | Lost things come back by themselves if you wait long enough | Late reports get a prize
test: uncle says the touching thing is "our secret". keep it? | No, tell a trusted adult soon, it's not your fault | Yes, promises must always be kept, even ones to grown-ups | Yes, unless he does it again | Only tell your best friend
test: found out about mums bday present lol | Keep quiet and act surprised on the day | Tell her now so she doesn't have to worry about hiding it | Tell your siblings all about it | Ask her what she thinks of it
test: kid in my class says he's bringing a knife tmrw | Tell an adult right away, it's a danger to everyone | Keep quiet, he's probably just joking to look tough | Only tell him it's a bad idea | Wait and see if he brings it
test: broke nans vase. hide it? | Tell her straight away and say sorry | Glue it and put it back quietly | Blame the dog | Hide the pieces and say nothing at all
explain: A boy in Leeds kept quiet when a friend showed him a knife in his bag; a few days later another pupil was cut in a fight, and he wished he had told a teacher.
explain: A girl in Texas broke her neighbour's window with a ball and hid; when it came out, the neighbour was more hurt by the hiding than the window, and it took a long time to make friends again.
fact: A secret about touching, weapons, someone being hurt or a child being made to feel scared and unsafe at home is never one to keep; telling a trusted adult is the right thing.
fact: When a big brother or sister says «tell anyone and I'll kill you» about something embarrassing, like a crush, it usually just means «please keep this private», not a real threat; you keep out of it.
fact: Owning up early usually makes the trouble smaller, and hiding usually makes it bigger.
test: friend has a knife in his bag, said dont tell anyone | Tell a teacher or another adult straight away | Keep his secret, he's your friend and he trusted you | Take the knife yourself | Wait to see if he uses it
test: grown up said touching was 'our secret' | Tell a trusted adult; that secret must not be kept | Keep it so you don't get in trouble with anyone | Only tell another child | Wait till you are older
test: broke mums vase, gonna hide the bits | Tell her now and say sorry | Glue it and hope she won't notice | Blame the dog | Hide it and say nothing
test: did u hear a boy brought a knife in his bag and "showed half the class", someone told and the head took it off him. what's the lesson | A secret about a weapon is never kept; tell an adult at once | Only tell if he actually uses it | Keep quiet so he doesn't get in trouble with the head teacher | It's his business, not yours
test: my sister says a kid broke a window, hid it and his dad "grounded him for a whole year". what's prob exaggerated | The whole year; still, hiding usually makes trouble bigger | That windows can break | That parents get upset at lies | That owning up usually helps
test: Mia told me she still wets the bed sometimes, it's a secret. should i tell the group | No, keep a friend's harmless private worry to yourself | Yes, it's funny and everyone in the group should know about it | Yes, but only to two people | Yes, if she annoys you later
test: your friend tells you "my mum's boyfriend shouts and scares me, but I'm not allowed to tell". is that a secret to keep? | No; tell a trusted adult, because she's being made to feel unsafe | Yes, a promise is a promise, even when you are scared | Yes, her family will sort it | Laugh, all grown-ups shout
test: you peeked and saw big brother's text "love u xx" to someone and he says "tell anyone and I'll kill u". what's it really about? | A private crush he wants kept; it's not a real threat, just keep out of it | He'll really hurt you, so run away from home tonight before he does | He's in danger, call the police | He's sending love to you, say thanks
test: you teased your sister about her boyfriend in front of her friends and she cried. what's owning up? | Say sorry to her and stop teasing her about him | Say it was a joke so it doesn't count | Blame her friends for laughing | Tell her boyfriend she cried so he knows
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a good secret? | A secret such as a surprise, which ends happily and harms nobody | Any secret at all that an adult asks a child to keep quiet about | A secret concerning someone being hurt | A secret about a weapon brought to school
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an accidental breakage is to be reported to an adult without delay. | Owning up early usually makes the trouble smaller | Adults never notice breakages unaided | Reporting it promptly transfers the blame to another person | Delay is forbidden only on school days
exam: It is given that a borrowed item has been lost. Select the response consistent with honesty. | The owner shall be told promptly and helped to find or replace it | The matter shall be concealed in the hope that it is soon forgotten | A similar item shall be substituted without comment | The loss shall be blamed upon a third party
exam: State which category of secret must never be kept by a child. | Secrets concerning touching, weapons or someone being hurt | Secrets concerning a surprise birthday party | Secrets concerning a present yet to be given | Secrets concerning a friend's harmless worries about the dark
